Danny what role did the original Blade  Runner play in your life no seriously  it's a movie that I discovered that at a  very precise moment when I was starting  to dream about the possibility of being  a director discovering what was the job  of a director and I think it's like for  me it's like a very strong example of a  strong director's vision and it's like  movie that aesthetically also blew my  mind at the time I was really hungry for  strong serious science fiction you know  and there's not a lot of very strong  sci-fi movies and and that one was  really really aesthetically blew my mind  and it's a masterwork  Harrison when you were sent the script  what was your reaction to the idea of  returning to this character I was  delighted to be involved once I had a  chance to read the script and see what  the opportunities were for taking  advantage of the audience's knowledge of  Deckard that there that came from the  first film and progressing the character  and and bringing that character into  relationships that I thought were  powerfully emotional and so it seemed  like a great opportunity at that at the  first first time I was involved Ridley  was meant to directed and then when  Denny became involved we met and talked  about it and I was really excited about  it  Roger I understand you and Denis had a  really kind of intense session creating  the storyboards for this film and in it  basically in a hotel room in Canada can  you talk a little bit about what your  early work early ideas were well it  started then  much longer process it wasn't like and  one intense session we started in like  September 2015 and went on to the like  the last day of prep before shooting so  it was quite a long process I mean we  started off just basically going through  the script and thinking well how are we  going to visualize this you know and  Dennis was on board and you know we just  gradually kind of worked it through we  all found different references of  different things and discuss different  ideas about the look and the  architecture and the atmosphere and you  know it's just you know spitballing and  they call over here Dennis California  and 2049 looks like a horrible place to  live how did you make it that way  when I first met Binney I asked him a  fairly simple question I said can you  give me one word that describes the  movie in and he paused and he said  brutality and I said okay great  that's a good start so we took that word  and we basically started with the  spinner because that was going to be the  the language that was going to reflect  on a lot of what the movie looked like  and obviously respecting the original  film and and we arrived at something  that I thought was was very compatible  but it was certainly singular to to  ourselves as well - so Ryan you were  right at home in this brutal landscape  what was it like to to work among these  among these sets yeah well I said to  Danny I asked him to describe all in one  word what it would be like and he said  there's snow  I said I said yeah yes yes that I can do  look this the sets were really the the  level to which they had committed to  this vision you know to walk you know on  to these sets and and you know I  remember one day there was a it was a  1/3 of a page it was like my character  walks up to a desk and I assumed that I  would just go to work and walk up to a  desk but they had turned an entire  soundstage into into a one desk it was  just it was so massive and after Roger  had lit it and it was it was a beautiful  but I was confused you know I said it's  a journey isn't this scene just you know  like 3/4 of a page you know and he said  but it's in the movie isn't it yes well  then it you know it has to it has to  operate under the same rules as  everything else so you know it was a it  was a real experience to to work on that  scale with this level of craftsmanship  and artistry and to really physically  inhabit this what felt like a  functioning universe in a lot of ways  Harrison what was it like to shoot that  fight scene in among the Elvis and  lights it was it was complicated I mean  that what I hadn't reckoned on was that  the lights would make it hard to see  where Ryan was  [Laughter]  and he he dances forward a lot better  than he dances backwards so I hit him  pretty hard one time you know it was it  was complicated complicated to fit in  between them and also to get a sense of  the hologram although we had a live  Elvis impersonator for our amusement but  it was fun it was fun  Ryan was it fun for you yes  Roderick this is a very big movie for  your company Alcon to make why did you  guys decide to bet on such a such a big  in such an ambitious film well I think  it was a combination of things  um one we never would have really jumped  off this bridge unless we come up with  what we thought was a very compelling  story to tell and a very a movie that  could stand on its own yet still live up  to the quality of the first film which  we we felt like the brand the brand of  Blade Runner is quality and and it set  so many standards in artistry and all  the rest of it that in order to actually  do it we were like we have to we have to  strive to be you know the best that we  can be so once once we had the rights we  went and saw Ridley and he was very  enthusiastic to jump on board he  certainly had been waiting for many  years to try to untangle  the rights and so he and Hampton then  got together and came up with the the  basic core of the story then Michael  Green came on wrote a screenplay and you  know Ridley had been speaking Harrison  periodically and then he read the script  and was on board so you know once the  train starts going it's it's it's it's  very exciting it's very exhilarating and  then really couldn't at the time that we  wanted to start he couldn't start and so  we all agreed to try to get another  director and we got didn't he  you know we had worked with Denis and  Roger on prisoners and you know loved  that expense one of our favorite  experiences and you know once we got the  the core together here it's been one of  the best experiences we've ever had on a  movie it was everyone's passion and you  know devotion even the Elvis was he was  all in everyone really cared about what  we were doing and and it's it's you know  and for us we felt like the art was  going to be the commerce so it just  needed to be a great movie you can't  tell what's CG and what's in camera what  was your strategy in terms of the visual  effects well the first thing that we all  decided the writer that starts that that  we will construct as much set as  possible so the actors will be inspired  by the set and they will have to focus  on the and their and on their character  and not on into the environment also for  I will say if I'm a Roger for for uni it  was like a way to will we storyboard  everything but we loved also when the an  actor bring an idea bring there's  something that sparks and and on the  morning to readjust ourself and and very  often there are strong images that are  coming spontaneously on set and I didn't  want when you are  this virtual environment surrounded by  green screens don't have that  spontaneity you don't have that  relationship with acting and cut the  camera and so it was important for me to  have and I'm very grateful that the  production decided to follow to support  that idea to construct all the sets also  it was a way to control the atmospheres  because we were constructing the sets  like an apartment for and as an example  that there's a window we will build the  other Street on the other side and then  the rain in between was be real so Roger  will be able to control the light the  light and in between and this way it  means that there's a tons of CGI in the  movie but it's always - and in the  background are a little elements that  are and I think that the fact that it  looks that real as there's two things  from is first of all all the artists CG  artists that work on this movie most of  them their Bible the way they were  brought to the cinema that's passion for  one of their favorite movie of all time  is Blade Runner also then their level of  commitment was so huge and the other  thing about CG is that at the end of the  day is light it's about light and the  way you lit with the computer and the  backgrounds like so it's all Roger  master you know the way blended the VFX  with that's our saying thank you so much  gentleman

